    <div class="prose">
      <p>Exterior cleaning lives and dies on being seen. The work is brilliant for it, a filthy driveway next to a spotless one stops people mid-scroll, but most exterior cleaners are stuck relying on word of mouth and the odd Facebook group post where they're one of ten firms racing each other to the cheapest quote.</p>
      <p>That's the problem. The people who message a cheap-quote post were never going to be good customers. Meanwhile the homeowner with a big render job and money to spend never sees you. Here's how I fix that.</p>

      <h2>Why exterior cleaners struggle for good leads</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Word of mouth is unpredictable.</strong> Great when it's flowing, dead quiet when it isn't. You can't plan a business around it.</li>
        <li><strong>The cheap-quote trap.</strong> Facebook groups train homeowners to price-shop, so you attract people chasing the lowest number, not the best job.</li>
        <li><strong>The good jobs are invisible.</strong> Render, roof and full-property cleans are where the real money is, but the homeowners who need them aren't posting in bargain groups.</li>
        <li><strong>Seasonality.</strong> Spring and summer are mobbed, then it goes quiet. Ads keep work coming in the slower months too.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the ads actually bring in</h2>
      <p>I target homeowners, not bargain-hunters, and the ads are built around the jobs worth having:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Driveway and patio cleaning</li>
        <li>Render and wall cleaning</li>
        <li>Roof cleaning and moss removal</li>
        <li>Gutter clearing</li>
        <li>Full-property exterior cleans</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why real footage wins this trade</h2>
      <p>No trade suits video like exterior cleaning. The before-and-after is instant and dramatic, and homeowners can't look away from a black driveway turning clean in real time. So we use <strong>real footage of real jobs</strong>, either filmed on site or cut from work you've already done, never stock. It looks real because it is, and that's exactly why it converts.</p>

      <h2>What the funnel asks</h2>
      <p>The ad clicks through to a page built to sort the serious homeowners from the browsers. For exterior cleaning it asks the things you'd want to know before you drove out:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>What needs cleaning, driveway, render, roof, gutters or the full property?</li>
        <li>Rough size, and the postcode it's at?</li>
        <li>When are they looking to get it done?</li>
        <li>Name and the best number to reach them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Someone after a cheap 20-minute wash won't bother. A homeowner with a proper job to spend on answers it in under a minute, and by the time it reaches you, it's worth quoting.</p>

